<%# This screen drives htmx, so the layout loads it. Set here rather than in the routes
    because here is where the hx- attributes are: a partial that stops using htmx, or one
    that starts, changes its own answer in the same edit. A route flag would be a second
    place to remember, and the failure it produces is silent -- buttons that quietly stop
    swapping and fall back to full page posts. The same shape as @charts, which every view
    that plots something sets for the same reason. %>
<%- @htmx = true %>
<%# The session, assembled from the two things a tap can change: the progress header, and
    the panel of the lift the tap was on. Both were one partial until #235, and a tap sent
    the whole of it back -- 127KB, 96% of the page, to tint one row.

    They are separate files now because the complete route renders them separately: the
    panel as the response, the header out of band beside it. Held together here so the
    first render is still one page rather than three requests. %>
<%== render('workouts/_progress') %>

<%# One lift a screen, swiped between, rather than five lifts down one scroll. Five lifts
    and twenty sets is a page nobody can find their place in: the lift you are on is
    somewhere past the ones you are not.

    Scroll snap and no JavaScript. A flex row that overflows is already a real scroller --
    momentum, rubber-banding and all -- on every phone, and snap-mandatory makes it stop on
    a lift rather than between two. A gesture library would have to be re-bound every time
    htmx swaps this partial out, and would be a worse swipe than the one the platform
    ships. overscroll-x-contain is the one part that is not obvious: without it a swipe
    that runs off the last lift is handed to Safari, which reads it as the back gesture and
    leaves the session.

    Restored to a stack from 640px up, and that is a decision rather than timidity. A swipe
    is a thumb; a mouse has no horizontal wheel, and the overlay scrollbar that would be the
    only other way across only appears once you are already scrolling, so on a desktop these
    panels would be every lift but the first sitting where a mouse cannot reach them. The
    column is max-w-md at every width -- a desktop was never shown five lifts side by side,
    it was shown the same narrow column -- so the stack is what a desktop loses nothing by
    keeping.

    The panels stay in source order and every one of them is full of buttons, so Tab still
    walks the session lift by lift and the browser scrolls each panel in as it goes -- a
    scroll container whose contents are all focusable needs no tabindex of its own. %>
<div id="lift-panels" class="flex snap-x snap-mandatory overflow-x-auto overscroll-x-contain sm:block sm:overflow-x-visible">
<%- session_lifts.each_with_index do |lift, position| %>
  <%== render('workouts/_lift_panel', locals: { lift:, position: }) %>
<%- end %>
</div>

<%# No copy of the rating scale here. The last lift panel's is immediately above this form,
    so a second one put two identical disclosures on screen with nothing between them but
    the five rating buttons, which is what #175 is a picture of.

    Nor is one rendered when no panel carried it, which is a session of nothing but
    warmups. A warmup is submaximal by definition and asks for no rating -- that is why the
    ramp rows have no RPE row and warmup-only panels have no scale -- so a session that is
    all ramp has nothing on it the scale would be explaining. %>
